Subject: [PATCH 3/5] test_0893 checks the repair's claims against a greedy
 reference

The hard-coded counts (1770 orphans, nnz 7260, 877 empty columns) came from
the Delaunay of a regular grid, which is not unique: another platform splits
the ties differently (1768 / 7264 / 871 on a Linux server). The test now uses
a seeded random cloud (no ties, a unique greedy result). It compares the
fine rows the repair rewrote with a brute-force greedy reference, the orphans
taken from the build without the repair. It keeps the 20 MB memory cap.

The reference is the only check on the widening: a query held at 9
neighbours passes every custom-MG test, and fails this one. Development's
builder makes the reference's claims too (1,876 orphans), and fails the
memory cap (59 MB). A second case has a single fine DOF: development raised
(IndexError), and the bounded query gives it to the first orphan.

The build statistics gain orphans_claimed, the fine rows given to orphans;
orphans_repaired keeps its meaning, the orphans found. The comment notes
the per-orphan loop is Python and that per-rank callers pass False.

Underworld development team with AI support from Claude Code
